  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nGenerally speaking, surface high pressure will remain in control\nof our area weather for the next week. The upper trough\nresponsible for bringing many locations their first fall freeze\nwill shift east today and tonight, and this will allow a quick- \nmoving shortwave trough in the Pacific stream to move across our \nregion. Given how dry the air is preceding the system, any \nrainfall will be light and mostly confined to northeast Louisiana \nand southwest Mississippi later this afternoon and evening. \n\nLooking beyond the near term system and through next weekend, \nstrong deep layer high pressure will become a fixture over the \nsoutheast CONUS and eastern seaboard region. This is a classic dry\nfall weather pattern, which offers little hope for significant \nrainfall any time soon. There are indications that a shortwave \ntrough crossing the Plains region next Sunday will push a weak \ncold front across the forecast area, but any rainfall associated \nwith this appears it will be light. Otherwise, daily temperature \nranges this coming week will be quite large (up to 30 to 40 deg \nF) in the dry and well mixed airmass. /EC/",
